  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-31845",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "The \"square of a difference\" formula states that\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2ax+{a}^{2}=(x-a)^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe will use this formula to factor the given trinomial. Our trinomial is\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+100-20x={x}^{2}-20x+100.\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the constant term as a perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-20x+{10}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 10x+{10}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor the trinomial using the square of difference formula with $a=10.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-10)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
